Arun Sharma <asharma@nospam.netscape.com> writes:

>kamal@wipsys.stph.net writes:

>> Hello there,
>> 
>> 	I have a problem related to shared memory.
>> Please see whether you could help me in getting over this.
>> 
>> In my program, shared memory call, shmat, is setting
>> errno as EINVAL(22), when called as
>> 	shmat(id,NULL,0);
>> 
>> These are some observations I made, while debugging.
>> 
>> * Shared memory with that id exists in the system
>>   and already three processes are attached to the same.

>How did you reach the conclusion ? What did ipcs(1) show ?
>See if you can get hold of strace or truss (I remember that someone
>had ported this to DU) and look for the exact system call being made
>and the arguments to see if something went wrong.

>> * This is the 30th shared memory being attached to
>>   the process. However, system's limit on shared segments per
>>   process  is set as 64.

Two things can be wrong:
  - 'id' is not an int or contains an invalid value. Since you've attached
    other segments (likely using this code), 'id' is likely correctly defined.

  - NULL != 0. Again, if you've used this call for the other 30 segments, then
    this should be OK.

Looking at the man pages for SunOS, Solaris, SysV88 and HPUX, the only reasons
EINVAL will appear are:
  - invalid ID
  - invalid ADDR

So something is wrong with either the ID or with the NULL. If those are definitely
both correct, then either the code is being munged, or there's a system problem.

I can't think of any other reasons....

Fest3er
