Rick Bronson wrote:

> Hi,
>
>   I have a PCI board with 16 interrupts that I'd like to map to 16
> signals (like SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, ... User-defined signals) but there
> aren't 16 of them.  Are there any other methods to accomplish this?
>
>   I'm using the latest Digital UNIX.

Ah, but there are 16 more signals for you to use, as luck would have it --the 16
realtime signals. They don't show up in <signal.h> as traditional
signal, i.e., SIGXXX. Rather, because of  POSIX, they're runtime-evaluated
using the expressions SIGRTMIN and SIGRTMAX. The Guide to Realtime
Programming in the doc set beginning with V4.0 has some examples of
how to use these signals. Also, O'Reilly's POSIX.4 book by Bill Gallmeister
has some more examples and explanation.

[Just FYI, on V4.0, SIGRTMIN is 33 and SIGRTMAX is 48. This could change
in a later release, which is what all the dynamic stuff is about, I guess. Of
course,
it's very, very unlikely that the definitions will change, but that's no excuse
for
hardcoding the definitions in your app ;^). (Well, let's put it this way -- I
left DEC in November, so it won't be *me* changing the definitions any time
soon!) ]

Jeff
