Before optimizing compilers and coprocessor hardware, a*a was normally
recommend over a**2.  However, that isn't necessarily true now.

The best way to answer this question, write it both ways in test program(s)
and time it.  With compiler optimizations turned off  you can get an
accurate idea of what the underlying hardware likes.  With optimizations
turned on, your code has to be structured to avoid getting optimized out of
existence.  Fill a large array with values, and loop over that.

On x86 platforms w/ 87 coprocessors the answer to the above tends to be
different than w/o a coprocessor -- similarly, the answer for integer
multiplication in the x86 vs. integers loaded into the 87 are often
different.  

On other platforms, there will be similar variations.
-- 
