In article <34BCD934.41C6@bristol.ac.uk>,
Arnaud Desitter  <Arnaud.Desitter@bristol.ac.uk> wrote:
>Yon Han CHONG wrote:
>> 
>> Is there speed difference between a**2 and a*a.
>> 
>No. A good compiler (and the digital's one is good in this respect) will
>use the best algorithm for a**b, i.e. a multiplication if b=2.
>
>Cheers,
>Arnaud

While it is true that the best implementation of a**2 is a*a on most
machines, there is no "best" algorithm for most of the remaining powers.
Implementing a**3 as a*a*a causes the expression to have a 1 ulp error
bound.  Implementing it as pow(a, 3.0) will cause it to have an error
bound of just over 1/2 ulp, assuming a good implementation of pow.

The other integer powers that have obvious implementations are -1 and 1.
The power zero has an obvious implementation that drives people who
think we are doing math instead of computing nuts.

					Sincerely,
					Bob Corbett



